{
  "dom_id": "step-q365430",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "| $x$ | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $13$ | $9$ | $5$ | $1$ |\n\nConsider the data sets above. You are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=6$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=7.$ Find the equation of the corresponding linear regression line. Round the slope and the $y$-intercept to **one decimal place** if necessary.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny=mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m:$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(3-6)(13-7)+(5-6)(9-7) \\\\  & +(7-6)(5-7)+(9-6)(1-7) \\\\  & =(-3)(6)+(-1)(2)+(1)(-2)+(3)(-6) \\\\  & =-18-2-2-18 \\\\  & =-40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(3-6)^{2}+(5-6)^{2}+(7-6)^{2}+(9-6)^{2} \\\\  & =9+1+1+9 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=\\frac{-40}{20}=-2.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =7-(-2)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =7+12 \\\\  & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=-2x+19.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
